WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.460
Film Courage: So during your two week notice
(I assume) were you planning out what your

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.280
day was going to be like once the six months
was under way?

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.300
Screenwriter Christine Conradt: Yeah, I definitely
had a plan and I knew that I wanted to spend

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:20.660
the time writing and I needed to figure out
what I was most efficient at I think and for

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.780
me at that time it was structure.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.500
I was always a really good character writer.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.950
So I knew I needed to learn structure better
and that was one of the things I was going

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:30.950
to have to work on better.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.269
I bought the books and so I did all that kind
of stuff and was definitely prepared during

00:00:35.269 --> 00:00:41.749
that time so I could jump off and begin my
six month education since six months actually

00:00:41.749 --> 00:00:42.749
ticks by pretty quickly.

00:00:42.749 --> 00:00:44.180
Film Courage: Yeah [it does].

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.639
I’m wondering what was day one like?

00:00:46.639 --> 00:00:49.139
Christine Conradt: Oh my gosh.

00:00:49.139 --> 00:00:53.989
I don’t even want to think about it.

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:59.839
I think there was a huge sense of relief in
it in that I was finally doing what I what

00:00:59.839 --> 00:01:05.300
I should have been doing for the last couple
of years and I felt like I was going to enjoy

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.770
every moment of this.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:13.560
I may only get six months of doing it.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.840
I just really enjoyed that this was the life
of a writer.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.780
Film Courage: And probably it was sort of
bittersweet, too because you thought maybe

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.270
that this was the only six months that you
would ever…that this would happen?

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.109
Christine Conradt: Exactly but I was thinking
that far ahead because I had a lot of confidence

00:01:27.109 --> 00:01:30.649
that I could make it happen.

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:37.140
So I don’t think I got caught up in this
might be the only time I get to do this.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:44.570
I was just thinking how do I make this last
and how do I make the money last so I can

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:45.880
continue to do it.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.170
Film Courage: How did you know you could trust
yourself?

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:52.229
Because I think that’s probably the number
one thing is knowing you could trust your

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:54.289
time and allocating the right resources.

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:58.390
Christine Conradt: I think I’ve just always
been someone who has trust myself.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:05.350
In that I guess when it comes to this kind
of a thing, the only one who is going to make

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.119
something happen is you.

00:02:07.119 --> 00:02:11.500
And I think just in my perspective you have
a lot of control over that.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.580
So I think for me, I’d gotten into USC.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.140
It was a very competitive program.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.980
I graduated at the top of my class.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.770
I had won awards while I was there.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.470
I knew I was a good writer.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.430
I wasn’t sure why I wasn’t working.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:27.610
Like I wasn’t sure why I wasn’t selling
scripts.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:33.080
And so for me a lot of it was like well it’s
not because you’re not a good writer.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.750
It’s because you’re distracted, you’re
not focused.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.170
So you’ve just got to get this back and
once you do, you’ll be able to move forward.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.620
And I think that’s where my head was at
the time.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:47.130
Film Courage: I think for so many people it’s
that big dilemma of “Do I want to be comfortable

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:51.740
and have a job and have one that has a good
title and that friends and family are going

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.340
to respect and that I respect myself for having.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.870
Or do I want to (unfortunately) have life
be a little bit uncomfortable?”

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:04.730
So were you prepared to be uncomfortable,
whether it’s cutting back on spending or

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:05.730
whatever?

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:09.510
Christine Conradt: Oh yeah…and I think I
still am to this day.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:16.880
I think I would tell any writer that, you
have to be willing to sell your nice car,

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.840
to sell your house and move back into an apartment.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:28.540
You have to love this job more than you love
anything you own because it can go away like

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.050
that.

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:34.240
Just because you have some success doesn’t
mean you should ever get comfortable with

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.850
it because things change, the industry is
always changing, you’re changing, you’re

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:43.310
getting older, there are new people coming
in, there is so much in flux all the time

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.410
that you really have to be in a place where
you’re willing to give that up.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:48.410
You have to love it enough.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:53.100
And if you don’t want it enough and you
really just want money, there are a lot easier

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.120
ways to go make money than to be a screenwriter.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.730
So that would be my advice.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:02.470
Film Courage: During the 6 months, were you
doing this seven days a week?

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.420
Christine Conradt: Yes.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:09.200
But it didn’t feel like work because I felt
like I was finally getting to do what I really

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.200
wanted to do.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.280
And I woke up every morning loving to write.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.790
I couldn’t wait to get back on the script
and keep working on it.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:17.790
So it never felt like work to me.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:21.790
Film Courage: So lastly if someone said to
you “Christine I know this is a huge risk

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:27.930
and I know that I shouldn’t take a line
of credit out or something else (use up my

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:32.190
savings, whatever it is) but I want to risk
it and I’m willing to face the consequences.”

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.840
What would you say are some hard and fast
do’s and don’ts?

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.090
Film Courage: That’s a great question.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:42.340
I would say go into it with a plan.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:47.810
You can’t waste a single minute of that
time, so go into it with a plan.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:53.130
And tell other people your plan, if this is
what you want to do…first of all if you

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:58.080
tell many people that you’re going to spend
your life savings to make a film or to present

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.420
to be a screenwriter for 6 months.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.300
Most people told me not to do it.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.580
They thought I was crazy.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:09.490
But I think you have to ask yourself those
really hard questions like how much do you

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:13.620
trust yourself, how much are you willing to
lose and give up.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:19.750
You can always go back and get a job at a
Y or a State Farm or wherever you were working.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:24.660
And in general successful people tend to be
successful no matter what they do, right?

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.220
So if you were being promoted at that job,
you can get another job.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.450
You’ll probably be promoted at that job
as well.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:35.790
But I would say go into it with a plan and
know what you want to get out of it and have

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.130
a way to measure that success.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:42.930
Because a lot of times I think writers, were
not very good a measuring what’s working

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.389
for us and what isn’t.

00:05:44.389 --> 00:05:48.100
We just sort of kind of willy-nilly just go
“Oh, today I feel like writing this.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.100
And now this script is done.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:53.240
And maybe tomorrow I will turn it in and maybe
I won’t.”

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.600
You have to really look at your life like
a business.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:05.900
And if you can manage it in that way, you’re
better prepared for seeing what’s working

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:07.389
and continuing on that route.

00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:11.889
Or seeing what’s not working an abandoning
that strategy.

00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:13.919
So you have to look at it more like a business.

00:06:13.919 --> 00:06:14.650
That’s a big thing, too.

